>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Whole vs Flakes vs Powder
    </nav>

    <h1>Whole vs Flakes vs Powder Dried Chilli: Which Form to Buy</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The same chilli variety performs differently depending on the form you buy. This guide compares
      whole pods, flakes, segments and powder across the dimensions that actually decide a purchase —
      shelf life, aroma, dispersion, colour and cost.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Buy whole pods for the longest shelf life and slowest aroma loss;
      flakes for visible texture and retained fragrance; powder for even dispersion and smooth mouthfeel.
      The form is a shelf-life and flavour decision, not just a texture one.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The four forms compared</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Form</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Shelf life</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Aroma</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dispersion</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Best for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Whole pods</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Longest</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Best retained</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">— (rehydrate / grind)</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Display, destination grinding, rehydration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Flakes / crushed (20–40 mesh)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Long</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Good</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Visible texture</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Seasoning blends, pizza flakes, garnishes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Segments</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Medium</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Medium</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Even</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Sauces, pastes, slow-cooked dishes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Powder (60–80 mesh)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Shortest</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Fades fastest</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Most even</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Smooth sauces, dry mixes, uniform colour</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the form changes shelf life</h2>
    <p>
      Grinding increases surface area, which exposes more pigment and volatile aroma to oxygen and
      light. Whole pods fade slowest; fine powder fades fastest. If your supply chain is long or you
      hold stock, buy whole or flake and grind at the point of use — or accept a faster colour and
      aroma fade on powder.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When to choose each form</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Whole pods</strong> — when shelf life and aroma retention matter most, or you grind
      and control the particle size yourself.</li>
      <li><strong>Flakes</strong> — when the finished product shows the chilli (pizza flakes, seasoning
      blends) and you want retained fragrance.</li>
      <li><strong>Segments</strong> — for sauces and slow cooks that need even extraction without a
      dusty texture.</li>
      <li><strong>Powder</strong> — when you need uniform colour and heat dispersion and a smooth
      mouthfeel, and you consume stock quickly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Cost and processing trade-offs</h2>
    <p>
      Whole pods are the cheapest to produce; each further size reduction adds a processing step and
      cost. But "cheapest per kilogram" is not "cheapest in the finished product" — if you buy powder
      that fades in storage, you pay twice. Match the form to your lead time, storage and application,
      not just the headline price.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, most buyers default to powder out of habit and then pay for
      it in colour and aroma fade — buying whole or flake and grinding closer to use is usually the
      better call for quality.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> Powder's only real advantage is even dispersion; its cost is the fastest
      degradation of the exact things you bought the chilli for. If you can manage a grind step, whole or
      flake gives you longer shelf life and better retained aroma for the same variety. Reserve powder for
      applications where a uniform, smooth result genuinely outweighs freshness.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a dried-chilli supplier's technical team,
      not a universal rule — the right form depends on your lead time, storage and application.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which chilli form should I buy?</h3>
      <p>Whole pods for longest shelf life and aroma; flakes for visible texture and aroma; powder for even dispersion and smooth texture. Choose by shelf life, application and stock turnover.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does powder fade faster than whole chilli?</h3>
      <p>Grinding increases surface area, exposing more pigment and volatile aroma to oxygen and light. Whole pods fade slowest; fine powder fades fastest.</p>
    </div>

